WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.852
Hello and welcome to mCoding.

00:00:01.852 --> 00:00:04.323
In what's currently&nbsp;my most popular video of all time,

00:00:04.323 --> 00:00:08.310
we compared the speed of a bunch of different ways to compute sum of range n.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.253
And a surprise to absolutely no one,

00:00:10.253 --> 00:00:13.540
the fastest way is, of course, to use the&nbsp;pre-computed answer.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:14.946
But to my surprise,

00:00:14.946 --> 00:00:18.423
I received hundreds of comments and even a pull request to&nbsp;my Github

00:00:18.423 --> 00:00:20.801
saying that the formula was wrong.

00:00:20.801 --> 00:00:23.977
All of the comments gave the same wrong answer.

00:00:23.977 --> 00:00:27.662
They&nbsp;all suggested that this minus should be a plus.

00:00:27.662 --> 00:00:31.710
But James, the sum of the first n numbers is&nbsp;n(n+1)/2.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.439
Here's a whole Wikipedia article on it.

00:00:33.439 --> 00:00:36.646
They're called triangle&nbsp;numbers and the proof is actually very simple.

00:00:36.646 --> 00:00:39.268
Make a triangle where the n-th row has n dots.

00:00:39.268 --> 00:00:42.713
Double the number of dots and flip them over in order to make a rectangle.

00:00:42.713 --> 00:00:45.258
It has height n&nbsp;and width n+1.

00:00:45.258 --> 00:00:49.303
The rectangle has n*(n+1) dots. So, the triangle has half&nbsp;that.

00:00:49.303 --> 00:00:51.585
Okay, okay. I admit that's a nice proof.

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:56.732
And yet, when we run the program, we see that's&nbsp;not the right answer.

00:00:56.732 --> 00:00:59.959
Try a different value, still not the right answer.

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:02.242
The correct formula&nbsp;uses the minus.

00:01:02.242 --> 00:01:05.859
And if you want to get an int back, so even the types match, then use integer&nbsp;division.

00:01:05.859 --> 00:01:09.227
One of these two factors is even. So, there won't be any rounding issues.

00:01:09.227 --> 00:01:13.178
So, how is it that&nbsp;with the correct answer right in front of them,

00:01:13.178 --> 00:01:15.784
hundreds of people not just caught the question&nbsp;wrong

00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:19.055
but all came up with the same wrong answer?

00:01:19.055 --> 00:01:23.783
A wrong answer that could be shown to be wrong&nbsp;by trying even a single example.

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:26.858
And it's not like these commenters are dumb or trolling or something&nbsp;like that.

00:01:26.858 --> 00:01:31.401
They're intelligent enough to know that there's a closed-form formula for this sum.

00:01:31.401 --> 00:01:33.622
And the&nbsp;overwhelming majority of them were not trolling.

00:01:33.622 --> 00:01:36.729
They genuinely believed that the formula was&nbsp;wrong.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:40.241
I attribute this to what I call 'Notational Fixation'.

00:01:40.241 --> 00:01:43.422
In simple terms, all these people knew&nbsp;the formula which has an n in it.

00:01:43.422 --> 00:01:47.098
But the n in the formula is not the n in the problem.

00:01:47.098 --> 00:01:50.428
The n&nbsp;in this formula is the biggest number in the sum.

00:01:50.428 --> 00:01:53.534
But the biggest number in this range is (n - 1).

00:01:53.534 --> 00:01:58.310
So, if you want to use the formula with the plus, you need to plug in (n - 1), not n.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:02.220
That terms this into n and this into (n - 1).

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.127
Rearranging, that's how we get the formula&nbsp;with the minus sign.

00:02:05.127 --> 00:02:07.221
In the time that I've spent teaching undergraduates,

00:02:07.221 --> 00:02:11.356
this 'Notational Fixation'&nbsp;has been one of the biggest sources of errors.

00:02:11.356 --> 00:02:14.773
It's what makes you think that this is a plot of y&nbsp;equals x squared.

00:02:14.773 --> 00:02:17.499
When, in fact, it's y equals square root of x.

00:02:17.499 --> 00:02:22.720
This is, of course, a primitive model of&nbsp;the number of xylophones over the course of years.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.167
And it's what might make you think that the area&nbsp;of this inner circle is pi * r squared.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:29.919
When, in fact, it's pi * r.

00:02:29.919 --> 00:02:35.393
Notational Fixation starts because&nbsp;it's an easy way to remember formulas and keep things straight in your head.

00:02:35.393 --> 00:02:38.389
Always using the same&nbsp;names for variables can be both helpful

00:02:38.389 --> 00:02:41.730
as well as a great tool for communicating your ideas with&nbsp;others.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.306
If I say, a squared plus b squared equals c squared,

00:02:44.306 --> 00:02:46.817
everybody knows I'm talking about a right&nbsp;triangle.

00:02:46.817 --> 00:02:49.220
But in the real world, problems aren't going to come to you

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:52.681
with their variables labeled&nbsp;like they are in your textbook.

00:02:52.681 --> 00:02:55.439
If you're worried about making mistakes due to Notational Fixation,

00:02:55.439 --> 00:02:57.478
then here's what I recommend.

00:02:57.478 --> 00:03:00.275
Practice scrambling all your variables.

00:03:00.275 --> 00:03:02.554
Make an x that's a function&nbsp;of f.

00:03:02.554 --> 00:03:04.725
Call all of your variables J.

00:03:04.725 --> 00:03:09.396
And of course, don't forget to try real examples that use good&nbsp;names and reasonable numbers.

00:03:09.396 --> 00:03:13.379
All these things will help you see past the notation and understand the&nbsp;meaning.

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:17.309
Just remember not to leave any scrambled variables in your code, of course.

00:03:17.309 --> 00:03:20.294
When you're&nbsp;communicating with others and with yourself in the future,

00:03:20.294 --> 00:03:23.641
it's important to use the notation&nbsp;that everyone's familiar with.

00:03:23.641 --> 00:03:24.718
Anyway, that's all I've got.

00:03:24.718 --> 00:03:27.696
As usual, thank you to my patrons and&nbsp;donors for supporting me.

00:03:27.696 --> 00:03:30.880
See you in the next one.

